                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi Der
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (Spec R, R) \to (\mathcal{M}_{1,1}, \mathcal{O}^{Der})
            %p
              is equivalent to the space of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+elliptic+curve"} derived elliptic curve
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            After we have looked at some concepts in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            a bit more closely below, we will restate this in slightly nicer fashion.
          %h2#references References
          %p A sketch of what this theorem means and how it is proven is part of the content of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Elliptic+Cohomology"} A Survey of Elliptic Cohomology
          %p
            and goes back to Jacob Lurie’s PhD thesis (listed
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} here
          %p
            The general theory for the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            invoked here has later been spelled out in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"} Structured Spaces
          %p
            The special case of the general theory that is needed here, where the coefficient objects of structure sheaves are
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectral+Schemes"} Spectral Schemes
          %p
            while the general theory of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            theory context needed here, is developed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} Commutative geometry
          %h2#NotionsOfSpace Notions of Space
          %p
            The statement that we are after really lives in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            (often called “derived geometry”). Here is an outline of the central aspects.
          %p
            The
            %strong central ingredient
            which we choose at the beginning to get a theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            going is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            whose objects we think of as
            %strong model spaces
            \: the simplest objects exhibiting the geometric structures that we mean to consider.
          .standout
